# Summary
Bug introduced. The new failure taxonomy correctly poisons on fsync failures, but it does not treat write_all errors after the file is opened as ambiguous even though partial bytes may already have been written. The safe behavior would be to poison the WAL-backed store, or otherwise repair/truncate the WAL tail, after any failed write attempt that may have modified the file.
In append_record_for_log_store, both length-prefix and frame write_all failures are mapped to BeforeReplayableRecord. However, std::io::Write::write_all can return an error after the underlying file has accepted a partial write. In the new WAL-backed log-store path, BeforeReplayableRecord causes apply_record to return an error without poisoning the store and without truncating the partial WAL tail. If the process continues and later WAL appends succeed, those later records are appended after the torn bytes and their corresponding mutations are published in memory. On restart, replay stops at or errors on the torn record before reaching later records, causing durable state loss or restart failure for the WAL-backed log store. This affects the newly added optional WAL-backed mode; the main durable control-plane path in the checked code still appears to use full restart artifacts rather than this new mode.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `80337741 Poison Raft WAL after partial write errors`.

The fix changes WAL length-prefix and frame `write_all` failures after the WAL file has been opened from `BeforeReplayableRecord` to `AmbiguousRecordMayExist`. That routes possible partial writes through the existing live-store poison path, preventing later WAL appends from being accepted after a torn tail. A regression test, `control_plane_raft_wal_partial_write_failure_is_ambiguous`, uses a writer that accepts a partial prefix and then fails to pin the classification.

