# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm write_all errors after opening the WAL are classified as BeforeReplayableRecord rather than ambiguous/poisoning.
- [x] Confirm apply_record handles BeforeReplayableRecord by returning an error without setting the WAL-backed store poison state or repairing/truncating the WAL.
- [x] Dynamically force a real partial write_all failure in the actual WAL append path, not just by manually corrupting a file.
- [x] Confirm the process can continue and a later WAL-backed mutation is accepted and published live after the torn WAL tail.
- [x] Confirm restart replay cannot reach the later valid record and instead fails/truncates at the torn/corrupt frame.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC plus LLDB trace. The relevant code maps both WAL length-prefix and frame write_all failures to ControlPlaneRaftWalAppendError::BeforeReplayableRecord at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4636-4647. In apply_record, that variant returns an error at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4383-4390 without setting inner.poisoned and without truncating the WAL. Replay is sequential and either truncates only incomplete tails or errors on corrupt complete-looking frames before reaching later bytes at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4705-4738.

PoC added a Linux-only unit test validation_wal_partial_write_error_leaves_unpoisoned_torn_tail. It uses RLIMIT_FSIZE to make the real WAL File::write_all(&frame) write exactly one frame byte after the 4-byte length prefix, then fail with EFBIG. The test then proves: (1) WAL file length is clean_len + 4 + 1, so a partial write occurred; (2) store.export_restart_artifact() still succeeds immediately afterward, proving the WAL-backed store was not poisoned; (3) a later RaftLogStorage::save_vote succeeds and is published live; (4) restart through ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::from_restart_artifact_with_wal_file fails with 'control-plane OpenRaft WAL frame checksum mismatch', proving the later valid record appended after the torn bytes is unreachable.

Command output: `cargo test -p storage validation_wal_partial_write_error_leaves_unpoisoned_torn_tail -- --nocapture` => `test control_plane_raft::tests::validation_wal_partial_write_error_leaves_unpoisoned_torn_tail ... ok` and `1 passed`.

Debugger evidence: LLDB breakpoint at control_plane_raft.rs:4386 stopped inside ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::apply_record on the BeforeReplayableRecord return path. Backtrace showed apply_record -> RaftLogStorage::save_vote -> validation_wal_partial_write_error_leaves_unpoisoned_torn_tail at the partial write failure point, confirming the real failing write path takes the non-poisoning branch. Valgrind and gdb were not installed in the container; LLDB was available and used. No memory-safety crash is expected because this is a durability/restart consistency bug, not a process-crash bug.

